<!--
  Robot configuration for the OctoQuad turret-encoder bench rig.

  THIS IS NOT A ROBOT. It is a spare Control Hub on a bench with a Digital
  Chicken Labs OctoQuad (FTC Edition) on I2C and two REV Thru-Bore encoders
  on the OctoQuad's pulse-width channels. It exists to settle the facts #169
  needs before a turret exists: firmware version, which bus, which channels,
  the pulse range, whether both encoders read cleanly, and how much the
  difference between the two moves per turn of whatever drives them.

  Like the robot configs, it ships inside the APK and appears in the Driver
  Station list as "robot_octoquad_rig" as soon as the app is installed. The one
  manual step, once per hub: on the rig's Driver Station,
  Configure Robot -> robot_octoquad_rig -> Activate. The OctoQuad Bench OpMode
  warns if some other configuration is active.

  WIRING:
  OctoQuad on the Control Hub's I2C bus 2, the same bus the competition robots
  will use for it (#174), so the bench facts carry over. Bus 0 is shared with
  the hub's own IMU; bus 1 is the Pinpoint's on the robots.
  Each REV Thru-Bore's ABS (absolute, pulse-width) output goes to one of the
  OctoQuad's channels 4-7, which the OpMode puts in pulse-width mode. Which two
  channels are a bench choice the OpMode reads from Panels, not a fact this
  file knows, so it is not written here.

  Device names here must match org.firstinspires.ftc.teamcode.hardware.DeviceNames
  (the OCTOQUAD_RIG list). RobotConfigXmlTest fails the build if they disagree.
  For I2C, `bus` is what the SDK reads; `port` is kept equal to it.
-->
<Robot type="FirstInspires-FTC">
    <LynxUsbDevice name="Control Hub Portal" serialNumber="(embedded)" parentModuleAddress="173">
        <LynxModule name="Control Hub" port="173">

            <!-- Turret angle sensing under test: OctoQuad on I2C bus 2. -->
            <OctoQuadFTC name="octoquad" port="2" bus="2" />

        </LynxModule>
    </LynxUsbDevice>
</Robot>
